[
    {
        "content": "<p>So in my never-ending side quest which I call combinatorial game theory I've found the need to talk about the profinite integers - specifically, I need the fact that the absolute Galois group of a finite field is Z-hat. How should we go about defining these in Mathlib? I know that there is a definition within FLT of Zhat as a set of integer sequences, but I've also been told that this is likely not the correct/Mathlib approach.</p>\n<p>Do we want to define <code>ZHat</code> as a direct limit? Do we want to define it as a special case of a more general profinite completion operator? What kind of API should we expose for it?</p>",
        "id": 577335941,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1772651658
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>cc. <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"816344\">@Aaron Liu</span> <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"110038\">@Kevin Buzzard</span></p>",
        "id": 577335960,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1772651664
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Can you define it as the product of the p-adic integers over all p?</p>",
        "id": 577336427,
        "sender_full_name": "David Ang",
        "timestamp": 1772651824
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I could, but there's like three other ways I could also define it</p>",
        "id": 577336471,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1772651838
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The question is not so much \"can I do this\" but rather \"what's the best way I can do this\"</p>",
        "id": 577336546,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1772651866
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>My own idea is that the most elegant approach would be to first define the <code>ProfiniteCompletion</code> of a group (as the direct limit of the finite subquotients), and then define <code>abbrev ZHat := ProfiniteCompletion ℤ</code></p>",
        "id": 577337298,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1772652112
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>(But I'm also very unfamiliar with the theory, so I defer to others' expertise)</p>",
        "id": 577337616,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1772652221
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>we have <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=ProfiniteGrp.ProfiniteCompletion.completion#doc\">docs#ProfiniteGrp.ProfiniteCompletion.completion</a></p>",
        "id": 577337618,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1772652222
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>but it's only in this category theory language and it's only for multiplicative groups</p>",
        "id": 577337723,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1772652255
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>...</p>",
        "id": 577337868,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1772652300
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>what's up with that? sometimes it feels like the category theory library is some second codebase orthogonal to Mathlib</p>",
        "id": 577337966,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1772652333
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Can we make use of this categorical definition, or will we have to do things from scratch?</p>",
        "id": 577338452,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1772652487
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>it's definitely usable</p>",
        "id": 577338511,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1772652505
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>oh ok I'm glad to hear</p>",
        "id": 577338531,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1772652513
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>you just need to unbundle everything</p>",
        "id": 577338549,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1772652521
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"816344\">Aaron Liu</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Profinite.20integers/near/577337723\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>but it's only in this category theory language and it's only for multiplicative groups</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>I guess everything in that file can just be tagged with <code>to_additive</code>?</p>",
        "id": 577338685,
        "sender_full_name": "Dagur Asgeirsson",
        "timestamp": 1772652566
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Do we want <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Profinite#doc\">docs#Profinite</a> in the root namespace to be the category, rather than some typeclass on groups?</p>",
        "id": 577338802,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1772652606
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>profiniteness isn't just for groups</p>",
        "id": 577338913,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1772652640
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>and you can also just spell it as <code>[CompactSpace G] [T2Space G] [TotallyDisconnectedSpace G]</code></p>",
        "id": 577339099,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1772652690
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Sorry, you can tell that I'm completely unfamiliar with category theory Mathlib</p>",
        "id": 577339325,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1772652755
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"459227\">Violeta Hernández</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Profinite.20integers/near/577337966\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>what's up with that? sometimes it feels like the category theory library is some second codebase orthogonal to Mathlib</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>What's wrong with just using the category theory library? Category theory is an amazing language for doing mathematics</p>",
        "id": 577339406,
        "sender_full_name": "Dagur Asgeirsson",
        "timestamp": 1772652777
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Mostly I'm worried about code duplication</p>",
        "id": 577340213,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1772653023
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Is it ok to have a file which defines the profinite completion of a group as some object in a category</p>",
        "id": 577340257,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1772653036
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>but then also have a file which defines the profinite completion of a group as a type satisfying certain typeclasses?</p>",
        "id": 577340303,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1772653052
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>should we use category theory to define the unbundled version, or use the unbundled version to define the category theory version?</p>",
        "id": 577340399,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1772653085
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>do we have pre-existing examples of similar design?</p>",
        "id": 577340443,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1772653098
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Category theory is awesome I agree, I'm just confused about how it fits into the rest of the library</p>",
        "id": 577340641,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1772653162
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>In general, it usually goes unbundled -&gt; category theory. But in my opinion, when a definition is most conveniently phrased in categorical language (like stuff involving limits, such as profinite completion), then there is nothing wrong with going the other way (and maybe realize you don't even need the unbundled version, and just work with the object in the category)</p>",
        "id": 577341134,
        "sender_full_name": "Dagur Asgeirsson",
        "timestamp": 1772653297
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>You're right, I guess the profinite completion is a very categorical construction</p>",
        "id": 577341399,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1772653391
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>So we have the profinite completion, we have the morphism into the profinite completion and the fact that it is dense</p>",
        "id": 577342052,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1772653658
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>How would we build the morphisms <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">^</mo></mover><mo>→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\widehat{\\mathbb Z}\\to\\mathbb Z/n\\mathbb Z</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.9289em;\"></span><span class=\"mord accent\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.9289em;\"><span style=\"top:-3em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">Z</span></span><span class=\"svg-align\" style=\"width:calc(100% - 0.1667em);margin-left:0.1667em;top:-3.6889em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span style=\"height:0.24em;\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"100%\" height=\"0.24em\" viewBox=\"0 0 1062 239\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><path d=\"M529 0h5l519 115c5 1 9 5 9 10 0 1-1 2-1 3l-4 22\nc-1 5-5 9-11 9h-2L532 67 19 159h-2c-5 0-9-4-11-9l-5-22c-1-6 2-12 8-13z\"/></svg></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">Z</span><span class=\"mord\">/</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">Z</span></span></span></span>? What's their generalization to the profinite completion setting?</p>",
        "id": 577342105,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1772653681
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>apply <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=ProfiniteGrp.ProfiniteCompletion.lift#doc\">docs#ProfiniteGrp.ProfiniteCompletion.lift</a> to the quotient map from <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">Z</span></span></span></span> to the finite (hence profinite) groups <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{Z}/n\\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">Z</span><span class=\"mord\">/</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">Z</span></span></span></span></p>",
        "id": 577342394,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1772653807
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Oh yeah I guess finite groups are in fact profinite yeah</p>",
        "id": 577342514,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1772653847
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Final question before I get to work, what would be a good file for this? Does <code>GroupTheory.Profinite</code> work?</p>",
        "id": 577343120,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1772654061
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Actually I do have another question: when we use <code>to_additive</code>, do we want <code>AddProfinite</code> groups or <code>MulProfinite</code> groups?</p>",
        "id": 577344260,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1772654441
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>(i.e. which do we treat as the default for <code>Profinite</code>?)</p>",
        "id": 577344281,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1772654450
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Am I doing this correctly</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Topology</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Algebra</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Category</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">ProfiniteGrp</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Completion</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">TopologicalSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">CompactSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">T2Space</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">TotallyDisconnectedSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Group</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">IsTopologicalGroup</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">in</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">/-- The profinite completion of a group is the direct limit of all its finite subquotients. -/</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ProfiniteCompletion</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ProfiniteGrp</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">ProfiniteCompletion</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">completion</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">mk</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 577345905,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1772655056
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Is this the Z-hat that's also in the FLT repo?</p>",
        "id": 577348612,
        "sender_full_name": "Ruben Van de Velde",
        "timestamp": 1772656063
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>yep</p>",
        "id": 577348704,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1772656092
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Oh, you said that and I can't read, sorry :)</p>",
        "id": 577348787,
        "sender_full_name": "Ruben Van de Velde",
        "timestamp": 1772656126
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Ok, so I immediately got stuck</p>",
        "id": 577349068,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1772656230
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=ProfiniteGrp.ProfiniteCompletion.lift#doc\">docs#ProfiniteGrp.ProfiniteCompletion.lift</a><br>\nThe universes of <code>P</code> and <code>G</code> are the same for some reason</p>",
        "id": 577349105,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1772656242
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Yeah, I get the impression (only from zulip, not from actually working with the code) that the category theory library often gets stuck with less-than-fully-general universes</p>",
        "id": 577349289,
        "sender_full_name": "Ruben Van de Velde",
        "timestamp": 1772656319
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I guess I don't need the universe generality to define ZHat since both it and ZMod are going to be in Type 0 anyways</p>",
        "id": 577349448,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1772656381
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>That can be someone else's problem</p>",
        "id": 577349486,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1772656391
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"816344\">Aaron Liu</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Profinite.20integers/near/577337618\">ha dicho</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>we have <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=ProfiniteGrp.ProfiniteCompletion.completion#doc\">docs#ProfiniteGrp.ProfiniteCompletion.completion</a></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>actually, what's up with this name? It mentiones both \"profinite\" and \"completion\", twice. Why not just <code>ProfiniteGrp.completion</code>?</p>",
        "id": 577350202,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1772656648
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>And why <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=ProfiniteGrp.ProfiniteCompletion.eta#doc\">docs#ProfiniteGrp.ProfiniteCompletion.eta</a> ? I think these sorts of maps are usually just called <code>mk</code> or <code>of</code></p>",
        "id": 577350429,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1772656734
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"459227\">Violeta Hernández</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Profinite.20integers/near/577350429\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>And why <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=ProfiniteGrp.ProfiniteCompletion.eta#doc\">docs#ProfiniteGrp.ProfiniteCompletion.eta</a> ? I think these sorts of maps are usually just called <code>mk</code> or <code>of</code></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>See <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/34893#discussion_r2777380997\">https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/34893#discussion_r2777380997</a>. But I agree <code>eta</code> isn't the best, maybe <code>unit</code> is better?</p>",
        "id": 577351093,
        "sender_full_name": "Dagur Asgeirsson",
        "timestamp": 1772657004
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>On mobile but I am pretty sure that there was already a long discussion about potential definitions of ZHat relatively recently.</p>",
        "id": 577351819,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1772657299
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>We discussed on the CGT channel for a bit, but I thought it'd be better to open a thread visible to more people.</p>",
        "id": 577351882,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1772657328
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"459227\">Violeta Hernández</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Profinite.20integers/near/577345905\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Am I doing this correctly</p>\n<p><div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Topology</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Algebra</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Category</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">ProfiniteGrp</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Completion</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">TopologicalSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">CompactSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">T2Space</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">TotallyDisconnectedSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Group</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">IsTopologicalGroup</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">in</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">/-- The profinite completion of a group is the direct limit of all its finite subquotients. -/</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ProfiniteCompletion</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ProfiniteGrp</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">ProfiniteCompletion</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">completion</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">mk</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div><br>\n</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>you shouldn't be assuming <code>G</code> is profinite, since the whole point of completing it is to make it profinite</p>",
        "id": 577353941,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1772658064
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>oh yeah, duh</p>",
        "id": 577353981,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1772658075
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>also it's not the finite subquotients, just the finite quotients</p>",
        "id": 577354141,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1772658132
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think there's probably also a version where G is a topological group and you take the discrete finite quotients, but in this case our G is just a group</p>",
        "id": 577354327,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1772658215
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"459227\">Violeta Hernández</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Profinite.20integers/near/577350202\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"816344\">Aaron Liu</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Profinite.20integers/near/577337618\">ha dicho</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>we have <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=ProfiniteGrp.ProfiniteCompletion.completion#doc\">docs#ProfiniteGrp.ProfiniteCompletion.completion</a></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>actually, what's up with this name? It mentiones both \"profinite\" and \"completion\", twice. Why not just <code>ProfiniteGrp.completion</code>?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>probably there are multiple completions you could want that give you a profinite group</p>",
        "id": 577355561,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1772658663
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>btw I tried to additivize the profinite completion, but it turns out that depends on additivizing <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=IsConj#doc\">docs#IsConj</a> which depends on additivizing <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=MulAut.conj#doc\">docs#MulAut.conj</a> and then it's just a mess</p>",
        "id": 577355977,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1772658829
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><a class=\"stream-topic\" data-stream-id=\"217875\" href=\"/#narrow/channel/217875-Is-there-code-for-X.3F/topic/ZHat/with/537326706\">#Is there code for X? &gt; ZHat</a>, in case it helps</p>",
        "id": 577359848,
        "sender_full_name": "Bryan Wang",
        "timestamp": 1772660268
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I would suggest <code>ProfiniteAddGroup</code> instead of <code>AddProfinite</code>.</p>",
        "id": 577360525,
        "sender_full_name": "Scott Carnahan",
        "timestamp": 1772660571
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>We already have <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=ProfiniteAddGrp#doc\">docs#ProfiniteAddGrp</a> (I just hadn't found it yet)</p>",
        "id": 577360703,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1772660633
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Also I previously did a fix for <code>to_additive</code> with <code>ProfiniteGrp.limit</code> <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/30341\">#30341</a>, which may or may not be related</p>",
        "id": 577360771,
        "sender_full_name": "Bryan Wang",
        "timestamp": 1772660664
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"519559\">Dagur Asgeirsson</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Profinite.20integers/near/577341134\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>(and maybe realize you don't even need the unbundled version, and just work with the object in the category)</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>How would you propose doing things like working with the Haar measure on <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat{\\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.9523em;\"></span><span class=\"mord accent\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.9523em;\"><span style=\"top:-3em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">Z</span></span><span style=\"top:-3.2579em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"accent-body\" style=\"left:-0.1667em;\"><span class=\"mord\">^</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> while working entirely in the category of groups?</p>",
        "id": 577368091,
        "sender_full_name": "James E Hanson",
        "timestamp": 1772663365
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>What's your point? I don't think I said anything about \"working entirely in the category of groups\". An object in the category of groups has a coercion to a type with a group instance on it, just work with that if needed</p>",
        "id": 577370322,
        "sender_full_name": "Dagur Asgeirsson",
        "timestamp": 1772664136
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Oh sorry, I think I misunderstood what not needing the unbundled version means.</p>",
        "id": 577371308,
        "sender_full_name": "James E Hanson",
        "timestamp": 1772664482
    }
